About code and working principle
# ESP32 Smart Heart Rate and SpO₂ Monitor

A compact, smartwatch-inspired health-monitoring prototype built using the ESP32, MAX30100 pulse oximeter sensor, and a 1.3-inch SH1106 OLED display.

The project measures heart rate in beats per minute (BPM) and estimates peripheral blood oxygen saturation (SpO₂). Measurements are displayed on the OLED in a simple interface, while live values and heartbeat-detection events are printed to the Serial Monitor.

The project demonstrates embedded systems programming, I²C communication, optical sensing, sensor data processing, and real-time display updates using the Arduino framework.

---

## 1. Project Overview

### What does this project do?

The system uses an optical sensor to detect changes in blood volume within a fingertip.

The MAX30100 contains red and infrared LEDs and a photodetector. Light interacts with the tissue and blood in the finger, and the sensor measures the returning optical signal.

The ESP32 communicates with the sensor, processes the readings using a pulse oximeter library, and displays the calculated measurements.

### Main features

* Heart-rate measurement in BPM.
* SpO₂ estimation in percentage.
* Real-time OLED interface inspired by smartwatch displays.
* Heartbeat-detection callback and simple animation.
* Serial Monitor output for debugging.
* Shared I²C bus for the sensor and display.
* Status messages for waiting, stabilizing, and displaying readings.
* Modular firmware that can be extended with additional features.

