## 3. Hardware Connections

### 3.1 ESP32 to MAX30100

| MAX30100 pin | ESP32 connection                        |
| ------------ | --------------------------------------- |
| VIN          | Voltage supported by the breakout board |
| GND          | GND                                     |
| SDA          | GPIO 21                                 |
| SCL          | GPIO 22                                 |
| INT          | Not connected; polling is used          |

The MAX30100 normally uses the I²C address `0x57`.

The ESP32 reads data from the sensor through the SDA and SCL lines.

### 3.2 ESP32 to SH1106 OLED

| OLED pin | ESP32 connection              |
| -------- | ----------------------------- |
| VCC      | Supported OLED supply voltage |
| GND      | GND                           |
| SDA      | GPIO 21                       |
| SCL      | GPIO 22                       |

The OLED uses the I²C address `0x3C` in this project.

### 3.3 Shared I²C bus

Both devices communicate with the ESP32 through the same two signal lines.

```text
                    ESP32 DevKit V1
                   +----------------+
                   |                |
                   | GPIO 21 (SDA)  |
                   | GPIO 22 (SCL)  |
                   | 3.3V           |
                   | GND            |
                   +----------------+
                       |  |  |  |
                       |  |  |  +------------------- GND
                       |  |  |
                       |  |  +---------------------- Pull-up supply
                       |  |
                       |  +------------------------- SCL BUS
                       |
                       +---------------------------- SDA BUS
                            |                 |
                            |                 |
                       +----------+      +----------+
                       | MAX30100 |      | SH1106   |
                       |          |      | OLED     |
                       | SDA      |      | SDA      |
                       | SCL      |      | SCL      |
                       +----------+      +----------+
```

### 3.4 Pull-up resistors

I²C uses open-drain/open-collector signalling. Pull-up resistors allow the SDA and SCL lines to return to a logic-high voltage when no device is pulling them low.

The project initially experienced unstable I²C scanning. Communication became detectable after adding two 2.2 kΩ pull-up resistors.

The intended arrangement is:

```text
             Compatible 3.3V logic supply
                       |
                +------+------+
                |             |
             2.2 kΩ        2.2 kΩ
                |             |
                |             |
             SDA BUS       SCL BUS
                |             |
         +------+-----+ +-----+------+
         |            | |            |
     ESP32 GPIO21  SDA OLED       SCL OLED
         |                          |
     MAX30100 SDA                MAX30100 SCL
```

Use one pull-up resistor on SDA and one on SCL when external pull-ups are needed.

**Important electrical considerations:**

* Both pull-up resistors must connect to a logic voltage compatible with the ESP32 and both modules.
* Do not connect the I²C pull-ups to 5 V.
* Some breakout boards already contain pull-up resistors.
* Multiple pull-ups operate in parallel, reducing the effective resistance.
* If the modules already have pull-ups, adding two more 2.2 kΩ resistors can make the combined resistance unnecessarily low.
* The appropriate resistance depends on bus capacitance, wiring length, device characteristics, and communication speed.

If the bus works with the current resistors, document the actual arrangement and verify the electrical characteristics before making the circuit permanent.

