## 5. Software Architecture

The firmware has four main responsibilities.

### 5.1 Hardware initialization

The ESP32 initializes the I²C bus:

```cpp
Wire.begin(SDA_PIN, SCL_PIN);
Wire.setClock(100000);
```

This configures GPIO 21 as SDA and GPIO 22 as SCL, using a 100 kHz I²C clock.

The OLED is initialized using the SH1106 driver:

```cpp
display.begin(OLED_ADDRESS, true);
```

The sensor is initialized through:

```cpp
pox.begin();
```

The SH1106 library is required because this particular 1.3-inch OLED worked with that controller driver.

### 5.2 Sensor configuration

The project configures the sensor's infrared LED current:

```cpp
pox.setIRLedCurrent(MAX30100_LED_CURR_11MA);
```

It then registers the heartbeat callback:

```cpp
pox.setOnBeatDetectedCallback(onBeatDetected);
```

The selected LED current affects optical signal strength and power consumption. A higher current does not automatically guarantee a more accurate reading and should remain within the sensor module's electrical and thermal limits.

### 5.3 Continuous sensor processing

The main loop calls:

```cpp
pox.update();
```

This is one of the most important parts of the firmware.

Avoid long delays, blocking operations, or lengthy display-processing routines in the main loop. They can reduce how frequently the sensor library is serviced and interfere with signal processing.

### 5.4 Periodic display refresh

The firmware uses elapsed-time checks based on `millis()`.

For example:

```cpp
if (now - lastDisplayUpdate >= DISPLAY_INTERVAL)
{
    lastDisplayUpdate = now;

    heartRate = pox.getHeartRate();
    spo2 = pox.getSpO2();

    drawMonitor();
}
```

This schedules display updates without using a blocking delay.

In the current implementation, the display interval is 500 ms.

The display update interval determines how often the OLED is refreshed; it does not determine the sensor's actual sampling rate or guarantee a new valid measurement at every refresh.

